<h2>Proofs about the simply-typed lambda-calculus</h2>
<h3><a href="uniqueness-example.php">1. Type uniqueness</a></h3> 
<h3><a href="path-example.php">2. Reasoning about paths in lambda-terms</a></h3>
<h3><a href="tps-example.php">3. Subject reduction</a></h3>

<p>
We first define a big step semantics for the simply-typed lambda-calculus using the familiar LF methodology.  We define first the type family <codeinline>notLam</codeinline> which allows us to check whether we encounter an expression which is not a lambda-abstraction. In addition we define the main reduction judgment as the type family <codeinline>step</codeinline>.
</p>

<pre class="code">
notLam: exp &rarr; type.
n_app: notLam (app M N).

step: exp &rarr; exp &rarr; type.

s_lam: ({x:exp}step x x &rarr; notLam x &rarr; step (M x) (N x))
      &rarr; step (lam T M) (lam T N).

s_app1: step (M' N) R &rarr;  step M (lam T M')
	&rarr; step (app M N) R.

s_app2: step N N' &rarr; notLam M' &rarr; step M M'
     &rarr; step (app M N) (app M' N').
</pre>

<p>To implement the subject reduction theorem, we typically state it as follows:
</p>
<p>
<strong>Theorem (Attempt 1):</strong> If &Gamma; &#x22A2 <codeinline>type_of M T</codeinline>  and &Psi; &#x22A2 <codeinline>step M N</codeinline> then &Gamma; &#x22A2 <codeinline>type_of N T</codeinline>.
</p>
<p>
We note however that the context &Gamma; and the context &Psi; are different; for the proof to go through we need some relationship between the typing assumptions in the context &Gamma; and the assumptions <codeinline>step x x </codeinline> in the context &Psi;. A simple way to achieve this is to generalize the theorem and state it with a generalized context which contains both, the typing assumptions about <codeinline>x</codeinline> and the assumption <codeinline>step x x</codeinline>, i.e, the variable <codeinline>x</codeinline> reduces to itself.  In addition, we keep track of the assumption that <codeinline>x</codeinline> is not a lambda-abstraction.
</p>
<p>
We therefore define first a context &Phi; which contains both these assumptions. 

<table border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=1>
<TR>
<TD> &Phi; </TD><TD> ::= </TD> <TD> . | &Phi;, type_of x T,  step x x, notLam x  
</TR>
</table>
</p>

<p>We now restate the subject reduction theorem with the generalized context &Phi;. 
</p>
<p>
<strong>Theorem:</strong> If &Phi; &#x22A2 <codeinline>type_of M T</codeinline>  and &Phi; &#x22A2 <codeinline>step M N</codeinline> then &Phi; &#x22A2 <codeinline>type_of N T</codeinline>.
</p>

<p>In Beluga, we specify the structure of the context using context schema as follows:

<pre class="code">
<keyword>schema</keyword> ctx = some [t:tp] block x:exp,u:type_of x t, v:step x x . notLam x;
</pre>
</p>


<p>The theorem is represented as a computation-level type in Beluga:

<pre class="code">
{g:(ctx)*}(type_of (M &hellip;) T)[g] &rarr; (step (M &hellip;) (N &hellip;))[g]
         &rarr; (type_of (N &hellip;) T)[g] =					
</pre>
</p>
<p>We make explicit the fact that the terms <codeinline>M</codeinline> and <codeinline>N</codeinline> can depend on the context &Phi; by associating both meta-variables with <codeinline>&hellip;</codeinline> which denotes the identity substitution on the context <codeinline>g</codeinline>. The type <codeinline>T</codeinline> on the other hand cannot depend on the context and hence it is not associated with anything. We read the type as follows: for all contexts <codeinline>g</codeinline> of schema <codeinline>ctx</codeinline>, if <codeinline>(type_of (M &hellip;) T)[g]</codeinline> and <codeinline>(step (M &hellip;) (N &hellip;))[g]</codeinline> then <codeinline>(type_of (N &hellip;) T)[g]</codeinline>.  The contextual type <codeinline>(type_of (M &hellip;) T)[g]</codeinline> directly describes the hypothetical derivation &Phi; &#x22A2 <codeinline>type_of M T</codeinline> where we make explicit whether an object can depend on declarations in the context or not.
</p>

<p>
The proof of the subject reduction theorem is then implemented as a recursive program. Case analysis and inversion in the informal proof are modelled via case analysis and let-expressions in the Beluga implementation. Appeals to the induction hypothesis correspond to recursive calls.
</p>


<pre class="code">
<keyword>rec</keyword> tps: {g:(ctx)*}(type_of (M &hellip;) T)[g] &rarr; (step (M &hellip;) (N &hellip;))[g]
         &rarr; (type_of (N &hellip;) T)[g] =							      
<keyword>&Lambda;</keyword> g &rArr; <keyword>fn</keyword> d &rArr; <keyword>fn</keyword> s &rArr; <keyword>case</keyword> s </keyword>of</keyword> 
| [g] s_lam \x.\v. \n. S &hellip; x v n &rArr; 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] t_lam \x.\u. D &hellip; x u = d <keyword>in</keyword> 
  <keyword>let</keyword> {F::(type_of (N &hellip; x) T)[g, x:exp, u:type_of x T]}
      [g, b: block x:exp, u:type_of x T, v:step x x. notLam x] F &hellip; b.1 b.2 = 
      tps [g,b:block x:exp,u:type_of x _ ,v:step x x. notLam x]
	  ([g,b] (D &hellip; b.1 b.2)) ([g,b] S &hellip; b.1 b.3 b.4) <keyword>in</keyword> 
  [g] t_lam \x.\u. F &hellip; x u

| [g] s_app1 (S2 &hellip;) (S1 &hellip;) &rArr; 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] t_app (D2 &hellip;) (D1 &hellip;) = d <keyword>in</keyword> 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] t_lam \x.\u. F &hellip; x u = tps [g] ([g] D1 &hellip;) ([g] S1 &hellip;) <keyword>in</keyword> 
     tps [g] ([g] F &hellip; _ (D2 &hellip;)) ([g] S2 &hellip;)

| [g] s_app2 (S2 &hellip;) _ (S1 &hellip;) &rArr;
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] t_app (D2 &hellip;) (D1 &hellip;) = d <keyword>in</keyword> 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] F1 &hellip; = tps [g] ([g] D1 &hellip;) ([g] S1 &hellip;) <keyword>in</keyword> 
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] F2 &hellip; = tps [g] ([g] D2 &hellip;) ([g] S2 &hellip;) <keyword>in</keyword> 
    [g] t_app (F2 &hellip;) (F1 &hellip;)

| [g] #p.3 &hellip; &rArr;             % s : step (#p.1 &hellip;)  (N &hellip;)          
  <keyword>let</keyword> [g] #q.2 &hellip; = d <keyword>in</keyword>     % q : block x:exp,u:type_of x t, v:step x x . notLam x
                            % since step #q.1 #q.1 = step #p.1 (N &hellip;) 
                            % N = #q.1  and #q = #p 
  [g] #q.2 &hellip;
   
;
</pre>


<p>One may wonder why we need to give a typing annotation to <codeinline>F</codeinline> in the case for lambda-abstraction. The reason is that we want to restrict <codeinline>F</codeinline> so it cannot depend on the assumption <codeinline>step x x</codeinline> and <codeinline>notLam x</codeinline>. Restricting what the derivation represented by <codeinline>F</codeinline> depends on corresponds to strengthening in the informal proof. 
</p>

<p>Otherwise the implementation of the proof mirrors closely the on-paper proof.
</p>


